{"slug":"express","topic":"what","note":"Express","noteTitle":"Express","section":"What Express is","text":"Node can already run a web server — the built-in http module does it in ten lines. What it cannot do is tell you which address was asked for, read the body of the request, or run the same check before forty different endpoints. Express is a thin layer that adds exactly those things and stays out of the way otherwise. In plain English Express is the receptionist for your server. Requests arrive at the desk; the receptionist checks who you are, decides which department handles what you asked for, and makes sure somebody always answers — even if the answer is no. ts A complete Express server It is deliberately unopinionated: it does not choose your database, your folder layout or your validatio"},
{"slug":"express","topic":"cycle","note":"Express","noteTitle":"Express","section":"The request and response cycle","text":"In plain English Every interaction on the web is a letter and a reply. The browser sends a letter — a method saying what it wants, an address, some headers, sometimes a body. Your server sends one letter back — a status code, headers, a body. One request, one response, and then it is over: the server remembers nothing unless you deliberately wrote it down somewhere. Every handler receives the same three things: the request, the response, and the way to pass along. The rule underneath all of it is simple and unforgiving — exactly one response per request . Zero and the caller hangs until it times out; two and Node throws “headers already sent”. json req, res, next ! The commonest form of this"},
{"slug":"express","topic":"routes","note":"Express","noteTitle":"Express","section":"Routes and routers","text":"In plain English A route is one line in a directory: a GET to /projects is handled by this function . A router is a page of those lines that you can move around as one piece. All the project routes go in one file, all the user routes in another. That way a big API stays easy to find your way around, instead of turning into one enormous file. A route is a method plus a path plus a handler. A router is a group of routes that can be mounted under a prefix, which is how an API stays navigable past about twenty endpoints. ts One router per resource, mounted under a prefix ! Order matters, and it is a real source of lost afternoons. Express takes the first route that matches. Register /projects/:i"},
{"slug":"express","topic":"middleware","note":"Express","noteTitle":"Express","section":"Middleware: the one idea to actually learn","text":"Middleware is a function that runs before your handler and can do three things: look at the request, change it, or stop it. It receives the same three arguments, and calling next() passes control to whatever is next in line. In plain English A parcel on a sorting belt. Each station can inspect it, put a sticker on it, or take it off the belt entirely. Reaching the end of the belt means reaching your handler — and a station that neither passes it along nor answers leaves the parcel sitting there, which is exactly what a hung request is. Figure One real request through this product's stack js Writing one The order they are registered is the order they run , and the stack in this product’s app."},
{"slug":"express","topic":"input","note":"Express","noteTitle":"Express","section":"Reading and validating input","text":"In plain English Input arrives in three places: in the address itself, in the query string after the question mark, and in the body of the request. All three come from outside your control, which means all three are claims rather than facts until you have checked them. Validation is where you turn a claim into something you are willing to act on. Input arrives in four places and every one of them is written by whoever is calling you: Where Reached by Notes Path req.params.id From the pattern — /projects/:id. Query string req.query.page Always strings, and can arrive as arrays if repeated. Convert deliberately. Body req.body Undefined unless a parser like express.json() ran. Headers cookies r"},
{"slug":"express","topic":"auth","note":"Express","noteTitle":"Express","section":"Authentication and authorisation","text":"In plain English Two different questions that get run together. Authentication is who are you? — checking the ticket at the door. Authorisation is are you allowed to do this ? — a valid ticket for the concert does not admit you to the dressing room. Systems that verify the first and assume the second are how one user ends up reading another user s data. Flow Signing in, then staying signed in Replay 1 Sign in Email and password arrive once, over HTTPS. 2 Verify the hash The stored value is a hash, never the password itself. 3 Issue a token Signed with the server key, carrying the user id and an expiry. 4 Client stores it And sends it as Authorization: Bearer on every later request. 5 Verify "},
{"slug":"express","topic":"errors","note":"Express","noteTitle":"Express","section":"Error handling","text":"In plain English Something will go wrong on every route you write, and you do not want the same handling copied into all of them. One error handler at the end of the chain catches everything, decides what the user is told, and makes sure the internal detail goes to your logs rather than into the response. An endpoint returns nothing at all — no response, no error in the logs — and the caller eventually times out. Where do you look first? Answer Two candidates, and both are in this note. Either a middleware neither responded nor called next() , so the request is parked on the conveyor belt; or an async handler threw without a wrapper, so the rejection went nowhere Express looks. Both produce "},
{"slug":"express","topic":"security","note":"Express","noteTitle":"Express","section":"Hardening an Express app","text":"In plain English A short list of things that are cheap to add and expensive to have skipped: security headers, a limit on how many requests one caller can make, a cap on body size, and never revealing internal detail in an error message. None of it is clever. All of it is the difference between a scan finding nothing and a scan finding a way in. Concern Answer What goes wrong without it CORS cors({ origin: [...] }) Either any website can call your API with a visitor’s cookies, or — set too tightly — your own frontend is blocked and the error names none of that. Headers helmet() Clickjacking, MIME sniffing, referrer leakage. One line covers a dozen of these. Rate limits Per-IP and per-account"},
{"slug":"express","topic":"design","note":"Express","noteTitle":"Express","section":"Designing the API","text":"In plain English An API is a user interface for programmers, and it is judged the same way: is it predictable? Consistent naming, consistent shapes, consistent status codes, and errors that say what to do about them. The cost of getting this wrong is not aesthetic — it is every future developer, including you, having to look up how each endpoint behaves because none of them behave the same way. Idempotent Running it twice is the same as once. GET, PUT and DELETE should be; POST usually is not, which is why double-clicks create two of things. Stateless The server keeps no per-caller memory between requests. Everything needed arrives with the request. Versioning A prefix like /api/v1 so a brea"},
{"slug":"express","topic":"testing","note":"Express","noteTitle":"Express","section":"Testing and debugging","text":"In plain English The useful test for an API is not does this function return the right value but if I send this request, do I get the right response . That kind of test goes through the real routing, the real middleware and the real validation, so it catches the wiring mistakes that unit tests step around entirely. An HTTP API is unusually pleasant to test, because a test is just a request and an assertion about the answer. curl is enough to start. js Poking at it by hand A request hangs forever — some middleware neither responded nor called next() , or an async handler threw without a wrapper. “Cannot set headers after they are sent” — two responses. Look for a missing return . req.body is "},
{"slug":"express","topic":"here","note":"Express","noteTitle":"Express","section":"Express in this product","text":"In plain English The same structure, in the actual API: where routes live, the order the middleware runs in, and where errors end up. backend/src/app.ts — the middleware stack in the table above, with the reasoning for each layer written next to it. backend/src/middleware/auth.ts — authMiddleware , optionalAuthMiddleware and adminMiddleware , including the re-read of the role from the database. backend/src/middleware/errorHandler.ts — ApiError , asyncHandler and the single handler at the end of the chain. backend/src/routes/ — one file per resource. Start with auth.ts , then projects.ts . Practice Write a twenty-line Express server with a GET /hello that answers with JSON. Call it with curl."}
